    {
      "name": "E multi-tab comparison",
      "model": "qwen2.5:3b",
      "status": "complete",
      "output": "The Garden pilot study, conducted on 42 volunteers, reported a 12% lower water use over six weeks without a randomized control group, suggesting potential effectiveness but with room for variability. In contrast, the Garden replication study, involving 210 gardens over a twelve-week period, found a 2% reduction in water use with a 95% confidence interval ranging from a 3% increase to a 7% decrease. This replication did not definitively establish a clear benefit, indicating limitations in its findings. Both studies contribute to understanding the potential impact of gardens on water conservation, with the pilot highlighting initial efficacy and the replication study introducing a level of uncertainty.",
